{"step_index":1138,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:39:11Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:39:11+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:39:11+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/LeanProject/GraphInvariants.lean`\nTotal Lines: 222\nTotal Bytes: 11395\nShowing lines 1 to 222\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: /-\n2: GraphInvariants.lean — a small, faithful Lean 4 / mathlib formalization layer for\n3: the graph invariants that appear in the *simplest* CEGIS survivors, so that the\n4: machine-generated conjectures are well-formed `theorem … := sorry` goals the\n5: DeepSeek-Prover-V2 backend (and the kernel) can actually act on.\n6: \n7: Scope is deliberately small. Invariants already in mathlib are reused directly:\n8: \n9:   order n            → `Fintype.card V`        (aliased `SimpleGraph.order`)\n10:   size m             → `G.edgeFinset.card`     (aliased `SimpleGraph.size`)\n11:   minimum_degree δ   → `G.minDegree`           (mathlib)\n12:   maximum_degree Δ   → `G.maxDegree`           (mathlib)\n13:   clique_number ω    → `G.cliqueNum`           (mathlib)\n14:   independence_num α → `G.indepNum`            (mathlib)\n15:   chromatic_number χ → `G.chromaticNumber`     (mathlib, ℕ∞)\n16: \n17: Invariants *not* in mathlib but faithfully definable in a few lines are defined\n18: here (domination family). Harder invariants (zero forcing, residue, Roman\n19: domination, …) are intentionally out of scope: conjectures referencing them are\n20: filtered out of the prove set rather than formalized incorrectly.\n21: -/\n22: import Mathlib\n23: \n24: open scoped Classical\n25: \n26: namespace SimpleGraph\n27: \n28: variable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n29: \n30: /-- Order of `G`: the number of vertices. -/\n31: def order (_G : SimpleGraph V) : ℕ := Fintype.card V\n32: \n33: /-- Size of `G`: the number of edges. -/\n34: noncomputabl\n<truncated 8875 bytes>\nes are even.\n191: Dropping it here would make this predicate strictly weaker than the hypothesis\n192: the pipeline validated, so a theorem proved against it would not be the\n193: conjecture. Caught by tools/lean_differential.py on the triangle-plus-isolated\n194: -vertex graph, whose degrees are 0, 2, 2, 2. -/\n195: def IsEulerianClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n196:   G.Connected ∧ ∀ v, Even (G.degree v)\n197: /-- `G` is nontrivial (at least two vertices): the graphcalc `nontrivial`\n198: flag, `|V(G)| ≥ 2`, i.e. `2 ≤ G.order`. -/\n199: def IsNontrivialClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := 2 ≤ G.order\n200: /-- `G` is connected. -/\n201: def IsConnectedClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := G.Connected\n202: /-- `G` is a tree: connected and acyclic. -/\n203: def IsTreeClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := G.IsTree\n204: /-- `G` is claw-free: no vertex has three pairwise non-adjacent neighbours,\n205: i.e. no four vertices induce a `K₁,₃`.\n206: \n207: Stated as a forbidden configuration rather than as `¬ (K₁,₃ ⊴ G)` so that the\n208: computable mirror in `GraphInvariantsComputable.lean` can be the *same*\n209: condition: the two files then agree by definition, with no unproved bridge\n210: lemma sitting between the statement we prove and the statement we refute. -/\n211: def IsClawFreeClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop :=\n212:   ∀ v a b c : V, G.Adj v a → G.Adj v b → G.Adj v c →\n213:     a ≠ b → a ≠ c → b ≠ c → G.Adj a b ∨ G.Adj a c ∨ G.Adj b c\n214: /-- `G` is a cograph: no four vertices induce a path `P₄` (Corneil–Lerchs–\n215: Stewart Burlingham). Distinctness of `a b c d` is implied by the adjacencies\n216: and non-adjacencies together with irreflexivity, so it is not assumed. -/\n217: def IsCographClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop :=\n218:   ∀ a b c d : V, G.Adj a b → G.Adj b c → G.Adj c d →\n219:     ¬ G.Adj a c → ¬ G.Adj b d → G.Adj a d\n220: \n221: end SimpleGraph\n222: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":1139,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:39:11Z","thinking":"**Understanding Radius and Exporters**\n\nThe `radius` in `GraphInvariants.lean` uses `mathlib`'s `SimpleGraph.radius : ℕ∞`, representing minimum eccentricity. This value is converted to `Nat` via `.toNat` for the exporter. A disconnected graph's radius is `⊤` which becomes `0` after the conversion. This explains the presence of `HasRadius` and its role as a hypothesis in the computable mirror.\n\n\n**Analyzing `Candidate` Conjecture**\n\nThe `Candidate` proposition tests a conjecture: if a graph is nontrivial and has a defined radius, then it also possesses a total domination number. Examining the definition of `radius` reveals its reliance on vertex reachability, which is checked via `ballWithin`. The `HasRadius` assumption is essential to avoid undefined behavior on disconnected graphs.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating Conjecture Validity**\n\nThe conjecture attempts to relate radius and total domination number. It seems that total domination implies the existence of a total dominating set covering the graph. Consider a graph and its radius. Any graph with a total dominating set has a total dominating set within the radius, or else it is not a total dominating set. The total domination is upper bounded by the radius. A total dominating set must exist within that radius; therefore, the inequality holds.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c '\\nimport random\\nfrom collections import deque\\nfrom itertools import combinations\\n\\ndef bfs_dist(adj, n, start):\\n    dist = [-1] * n\\n    dist[start] = 0\\n    q = deque([start])\\n    while q:\\n        u = q.popleft()\\n        for v in adj[u]:\\n            if dist[v] == -1:\\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\\n                q.append(v)\\n    return dist\\n\\nrandom.seed(42)\\nfailed = False\\nfor trial in range(200):\\n    n = random.randint(4, 9)\\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\\n            if random.random() < 0.3:\\n                adj[i].append(j)\\n                adj[j].append(i)\\n    d0 = bfs_dist(adj, n, 0)\\n    if any(x == -1 for x in d0):\\n        continue\\n    \\n    dist = [bfs_dist(adj, n, i) for i in range(n)]\\n    \\n    for k in range(2, n):\\n        for s in combinations(range(n), k):\\n            # check if G[s] has no isolated vertex\\n            s_set = set(s)\\n            has_no_isolated = True\\n            for u in s:\\n                if not any(v in s_set for v in adj[u]):\\n                    has_no_isolated = False\\n                    break\\n            if has_no_isolated:\\n                # check if exists u in V with max_{w in s} dist[u][w] <= k - 1\\n                ok = any(max(dist[u][w] for w in s) <= k - 1 for u in range(n))\\n                if not ok:\\n                    print(f\\\"FAILED for subset without isolated vertices: n={n}, k={k}, s={s}\\\")\\n                    failed = True\\n                    break\\n        if failed:\\n            break\\n    if failed:\\n        break\\nif not failed:\\n    print(\\\"HOLDS FOR ALL SUBSETS WITHOUT ISOLATED VERTICES!\\\")\\n'\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"10000","toolAction":"\"Testing subsets without isolated vertices\"","toolSummary":"\"Test subsets with no isolated vertices\""}}]}
